#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152937} :activity/id #uuid "8c455e50-7e18-5972-96ce-f524d3562bd6" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049552 :topic/id #uuid "fec793da-c490-5883-8ef3-12fbd0ff56e1" :topic/math-academy-id 2791} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152937 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153429 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we'll learn how to negate statements involving universal and existential quantifiers.\n\nTo negate the universally quantified statement $$\\forall x \\in U, \\, P(x)$$ we flip the quantifier to existential and negate the predicate: $$\\neg \\left( {\\color{blue}\\forall} x \\in U, \\, {\\color{red}P(x)} \\right) \\equiv {\\color{blue}\\exists} x \\in U, \\, \\neg {\\color{red}P(x)}$$\n\nTo see why it makes sense to do this, let's negate the following statement:\n\n$\\qquad$ *All integers are even.*\n\nThis statement can be re-phrased as follows:\n\n$\\qquad$ *For all* $n \\in \\mathbb{Z},$ $\\left(2 \\mid n\\right).$\n\nIntroducing the universal quantifier, we get the following:\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\forall n \\in \\mathbb{Z}, \\, \\left(2 \\mid n \\right)$\n\nNegating this according to the above rule, we obtain\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}¬(∀n\\in Z,\\,(2∣n)) & ≡∃n\\in Z,\\,¬(2∣n) \\\\ & ≡∃n\\in Z,\\,(2∤n).\\end{aligned}$$\n\nThis statement now reads\n\n$\\qquad$ *There exists $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}$ such that $n$ is not even.*\n\nWe can write this in more everyday English in the following ways:\n\n- *There is at least one odd integer.*\n- *Some integers are odd.*\n\nThis makes intuitive sense. The statement that \"all integers are even\" is false, and the reason it's false is that at least one integer is odd." :tutorial/id #uuid "0e092b7f-a329-5fcb-a336-d78dc61cabcd" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7996 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "285e0af3-92ec-5d9b-93f0-e95bc557ee46" :step/math-academy-id 145834 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152938}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152938 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153474 :tutorial/content "To negate the existentially quantified statement $$\\exists x \\in U, \\, P(x)$$ we flip the quantifier to universal and negate the predicate: $$\\neg \\left( {\\color{blue}\\exists} x \\in U, \\, {\\color{red}P(x)} \\right) \\equiv {\\color{blue}\\forall} x \\in U, \\, \\neg {\\color{red}P(x)}$$\n\nTo see why it makes sense to do this, let's negate the following statement:\n\n$\\qquad$ *There is a real number whose square is negative.*\n\nThe given statement can be re-phrased as follows:\n\n$\\qquad$ *There exists* $x \\in \\mathbb{R},\\, x^2 < 0.$\n\nIntroducing the existential quantifier, we get the following:\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\exists x \\in \\mathbb{R},\\, x^2 < 0.$\n\nThis statement is false, and so its negation must be true.\n\nNegating this according to the above rule, we obtain\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}¬(∃x\\in R,\\,{x}^{2}<0) & ≡∀x\\in R,\\,¬({x}^{2}<0) \\\\ & ≡∀x\\in R,\\,{x}^{2}\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nThis statement now reads\n\n$\\qquad$ *For all $x \\in \\mathbb{R},$ $x^2\\geq 0$.*\n\nWe can write this in more everyday English as follows:\n\n$\\qquad$ *The square of every real number is nonnegative*.\n\nThis makes intuitive sense. The statement \"there is a real number whose square is negative\" is false, and the reason it's false is that *every* real number has a nonnegative square." :tutorial/id #uuid "281fab10-7cea-51bf-b8f3-5a343f4c35be" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9151 :tutorial/title "Negating a Statement Involving an Existential Quantifier"} :step/id #uuid "95fe8ca9-1475-51f0-b13c-158949daff94" :step/math-academy-id 145835 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152939}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152939 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152084 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151808 :question/id #uuid "fdf5619d-5630-50e9-a603-2ba251002848" :question/math-academy-id "e-12743"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4598f8c6-29a4-5f54-b333-609d74d34044" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050878}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152467} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152468} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384243} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384811} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384818} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384825} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390427}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Formula For Negating a Quantified Statement"} :step/id #uuid "02e20143-516d-5a40-9287-934a0a3f86ff" :step/math-academy-id 145836 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152940}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152940 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152085 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151809 :question/id #uuid "349873e3-3d3e-5603-b5cd-540540e5a3cb" :question/math-academy-id "e-12744"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f58690c6-735d-5579-a228-58b5fdedcdab" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152441} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152445} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384250} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384832} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384896} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401756} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401763} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410142} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410145}] :knowledge-point/title "Negating Universal Statements"} :step/id #uuid "9d9db744-c4e7-5f5c-8d66-1a218dd923de" :step/math-academy-id 145839 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152941}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152941 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152086 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151810 :question/id #uuid "50bf2e82-cec4-5595-b0f8-ae546685413c" :question/math-academy-id "e-12745"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eb609a7e-467e-5003-b4c4-2a1637977443" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152442} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152446} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384903} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384910} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401770} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401880} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410141} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410143} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410144}] :knowledge-point/title "Negating Existential Statements"} :step/id #uuid "a0d422a5-1f67-5955-b8c0-fd3a59dc347b" :step/math-academy-id 145843}] :activity/title "Negating Quantified Statements" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186152375}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046673} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410146} :activity/id #uuid "894f8871-df27-5432-8273-79e87d1dc068" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049552 :topic/id #uuid "fec793da-c490-5883-8ef3-12fbd0ff56e1" :topic/math-academy-id 2791} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410146 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410142 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29f9e9a8-acf7-56b9-9960-2cbe3a46c2a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "There exists $i\\in Z$ such that $5∣i.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410114} {:answer/id #uuid "321e31d8-c886-5941-ae25-4240bbb508be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "There exists $i\\in Z$ such that $5∤i.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410115} {:answer/id #uuid "59888688-cf22-51cc-9fbe-28615559de4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410123} {:answer/id #uuid "846635d0-43bd-5c1e-aad9-f8614f81020b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "For all $i\\in Z,$ $5∣i.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410128} {:answer/id #uuid "9ecf36dc-e0ba-5908-96c8-ed866e4b2b75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "For all $i\\in Z,$ $5∤i.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410131}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410114} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a1d23f2-86cc-5a43-ad48-df426171ab05" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410136}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4e77b832-cd26-598b-a9cd-768bdf101358" :question/math-academy-id "q-183455" :question/problem "Negate the following statement:\n\n$\\,$ For all $i\\in Z,$ $5∤i.$\n\n*Hint: Recall that $a∣b$ means \"$a$ divides $b.$\"*" :question/worked-solution "If we denote\n\n$$\nP(i):5∣i,\n$$\n\nour statement can be written as\n\n$$\n∀i\\in Z,\\,¬P(i).\n$$\n\nNegating this, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(∀i\\in Z,\\,¬P(i)) & ≡∃i\\in Z,\\,¬¬P(i) \\\\  & ≡∃i\\in Z,\\,\\text{ }P(i).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $∃i\\in Z,\\,P(i)$ reads as follows:\n\n$\\,$ **There exists** $i\\in Z$ such that $5∣i.$"} :step/id #uuid "572c75d6-a924-55fc-82ec-9c67ff02ed45" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410150}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410147 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410143 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d27b4f1-4010-5f2d-a415-92176340a182" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "There exists $k\\in Z$ such that $2k$ is not even." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410118} {:answer/id #uuid "4c4dfbe5-97a5-50aa-a40f-1414bdfbd455" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "For all $k\\in Z,$ $2k$ is not even." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410122} {:answer/id #uuid "8d3dc0c0-bf2c-54cd-9809-125cf9be042b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the listed." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410129} {:answer/id #uuid "d2a530e2-fae5-5975-af00-86861b170c9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "For all $k\\in Z,$ $2k$ is even." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410134} {:answer/id #uuid "e8a52516-436b-5bd7-a39f-f1990b4cc0fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "There exists $k\\in Z$ such that $2k$ is even." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410135}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410134} :answer-field/id #uuid "a038f0d4-34cb-521f-89be-3859465a2114" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410138}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "798a00fa-243f-50df-8297-ecf784686589" :question/math-academy-id "q-216686" :question/problem "Negate the following statement:\n\n$\\,$ There exists $k\\in Z$ such that $2k$ is not even." :question/worked-solution "If we denote\n\n$$\nP(k):2k\\text{ is even},\n$$\n\nour statement can be written as\n\n$$\n∃k\\in Z,\\,¬P(k).\n$$\n\nNegating this, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(∃k\\in Z,\\,¬P(k)) & ≡∀k\\in Z,\\,¬¬P(k) \\\\  & ≡∀k\\in Z,\\,P(k).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $∀k\\in Z,\\,P(k)$ reads as follows:\n\n$\\,$ **For all** $k\\in Z,$ $2k$ is even."} :step/id #uuid "8695aa81-39f5-5e81-8b64-009141aabaf1"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410148 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410144 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17e49909-3507-5580-943d-286759dd3c72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "All rational numbers are proper fractions." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410111} {:answer/id #uuid "3f9e815f-a702-5472-ae0a-664589aeb164" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "All rational numbers are not proper fractions." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410119} {:answer/id #uuid "401e5e96-f94c-5f3b-9c08-dfc54196c291" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Some rational numbers are not proper fractions." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410120} {:answer/id #uuid "72b76584-0a79-5013-9010-9e565d577099" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Some rational numbers are proper fractions." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410127} {:answer/id #uuid "9f968828-8126-5609-8734-f89404875683" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410132}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410111} :answer-field/id #uuid "54cd8eb8-4350-5d38-9526-de55f16c63d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410137}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b61a6bc3-02ad-503d-9726-3870de55612a" :question/math-academy-id "q-183651" :question/problem "Negate the following statement: \n\n$\\,$ Some rational numbers are not proper fractions." :question/worked-solution "The given statement can be re-phrased as follows:\n\n$\\,$ There exists $x\\in Q$ such that $x$ is not a proper fraction.\n\nIntroducing the existential quantifier, we get the following:\n\n$\\,$ $∃x\\in Q,\\,x\\text{ is not a proper fraction}.$\n\nNegating this, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(∃x\\in Q,\\,x\\text{ is not a proper fraction}) & ≡∀x\\in Q,\\,¬(x\\text{ is not a proper fraction}) \\\\  & ≡∀x\\in Q,\\,x\\text{ is a proper fraction}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich reads \n\n$\\,$ For all $x\\in Q,$ $x\\text{ is a proper fraction}.$\n\nor simply\n\n$\\,$ All rational numbers are proper fractions."} :step/id #uuid "e053a657-a0c4-5b0c-be02-023521cbea13" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410149}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410149 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410145 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25b9afc4-148f-5c71-8511-e46ca1e987c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "For all $m\\in N,$ $m$ is odd." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410113} {:answer/id #uuid "3ce9128f-5377-56ee-920f-b7ab48ecc8f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the listed." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410117} {:answer/id #uuid "60188a81-7597-5502-b7d3-b21f7efe67e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "There exists $m\\in N$ such that $3m+1$ is odd." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410124} {:answer/id #uuid "717466a3-482b-5b78-832a-f3993143c94b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "There exists $m\\in N$ such that $3m+1$ is not odd." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410126} {:answer/id #uuid "a9e42e20-34f7-5639-b41e-d4a2e24a21c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "For all $m\\in N,$ $m$ is not odd." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410133}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410124} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7c5ef5b-7a80-5ac2-99a7-b5d9b6b74e69" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410140}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c83b3bea-4b77-5cec-bbd2-330b49e09c81" :question/math-academy-id "q-216590" :question/problem "Negate the following statement:\n\n$\\,$ For all $m\\in N,$ $3m+1$ is not odd." :question/worked-solution "If we denote\n\n$$\nP(m):3m+1\\text{ is odd},\n$$\n\nour statement can be written as\n\n$$\n∀m\\in N,\\,¬P(m).\n$$\n\nNegating this, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(∀m\\in N,\\,¬P(m)) & ≡∃m\\in N,\\,¬¬P(m) \\\\  & ≡∃m\\in N,\\,P(m).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $∃m\\in N,\\,P(m)$ reads as follows:\n\n$\\,$ **There exists** $m\\in N$ such that $3m+1$ is odd."} :step/id #uuid "e973016a-86f0-5ff0-b7fb-787bf770612e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410147}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410150 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410141 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "251a042a-d8c4-506c-af57-a854707853ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "At least one prime number is even." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410112} {:answer/id #uuid "3c45df10-d57c-50a6-a771-4be55ec10faa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Every prime number is even." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410116} {:answer/id #uuid "418005b2-29df-534e-be9b-4ebcd2a4e0a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410121} {:answer/id #uuid "66acba5e-e759-57f7-ba26-fcd18beb17b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "At least one prime number is odd." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410125} {:answer/id #uuid "927517ce-f64f-517e-8127-f452cd455459" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Every prime number is odd." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410130}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410130} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5ddecd2-3447-559e-ad3a-01957e0b66df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410139}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1b470ce3-7367-5e3f-ae84-b975432e9387" :question/math-academy-id "q-183471" :question/problem "Negate the following statement: \n\n$\\,$ At least one prime number is even." :question/worked-solution "Let $P$ be the set of all prime numbers. The given statement can be re-phrased as follows:\n\n$\\,$ There exists $p\\in P$ such that $p$ is even.\n\nIntroducing the existential quantifier, we get the following:\n\n$\\,$ $∃p\\in P,\\,p\\text{ is even}.$\n\nNegating this, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(∃p\\in P,\\,p\\text{ is even}) & ≡∀p\\in P,\\,¬(p\\text{ is even}) \\\\  & ≡∀p\\in P,\\,p\\text{ is odd}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich reads \n\n$\\,$ For all $p\\in P,$ $p$ is odd.\n\nor simply\n\n$\\,$ Every prime number is odd."} :step/id #uuid "f94ff61b-9b2f-5d75-b6b3-db067a45dad0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410148}}] :activity/title "Negating Quantified Statements" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410110}]}
